<h2>Hurricane, Windstorm, and TWIA Claims</h2>
<p>The Coastal Bend's hurricane exposure is part of daily life here, from Celia in 1970 to Harvey in 2017 and Hanna in 2020. Hurricane claims often involve wind, wind-driven rain, and storm surge at once, and each may fall under a different policy. Many coastal owners have a homeowners policy that excludes wind and hail, a TWIA windstorm policy, and a separate flood policy, and wind-versus-water disputes are common. See <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> for how those claims are handled and the statutory TWIA deadlines.</p>

<h2>Hail, Wind, Roof, and Storm Damage</h2>
<p>NOAA storm records show Nueces County hail peaking in April and May, and severe thunderstorm winds can be extreme: the May 8, 2025 windstorm produced NWS-estimated gusts of 105 to 115 mph around Petronila, Driscoll, and Agua Dulce. Our storm-related services include <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a>, <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a>, <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>, and <a href="/services/storm-damage-claims/">storm damage claims</a> that involve several causes at once. The <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> can help you find reported weather events near your property.</p>

<h2>Fire and Smoke Damage</h2>
<p>Fire damage does not end when the flames are out. Smoke, soot, and heat can affect framing, wiring, HVAC systems, insulation, and contents in ways that are not visible at first, and water used to fight the fire adds another layer of damage. We work on structural fire damage, smoke and soot contamination, odor problems, water damage from firefighting, and partial or total losses. See <a href="/services/fire-damage-claims/">fire damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/smoke-damage-claims/">smoke damage claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Water, Flood, and Mold Claims</h2>
<p>Water losses are among the most misunderstood claims because coverage often turns on where the water came from and how quickly the damage happened. We assist with storm-related water intrusion through damaged roofs and windows, burst pipes and plumbing failures (including freeze breaks like those in February 2021), roof leaks, mold that develops as secondary damage where the policy covers it, and flood damage under a separate flood policy. Standard homeowners and commercial policies generally exclude flood; flood coverage usually comes from the NFIP or a private flood insurer, and new NFIP policies generally have a 30-day waiting period. Learn more on our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage</a>, <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage</a>, and <a href="/services/mold-damage-claims/">mold damage</a> claims pages.</p>

<h2>Other Covered Property Losses</h2>
<p>Not every loss fits neatly into one category. We also review claims involving structural collapse, theft and vandalism, vehicle or tree impact, and damage to equipment and contents. If you are not sure whether a loss is covered, we can read your policy with you and explain your options before a costly mistake is made.</p>

