<h2>Reopening Previously Settled Claims</h2>
<p>Many policyholders assume a closed claim is final. Often it is not. Depending on the policy and the timeline, a claim may be supplemented or reopened when additional damage surfaced later, repairs uncovered hidden damage, the original pricing or scope was incomplete, or the payment did not reflect real repair costs. Our <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a> service addresses these situations. When the disagreement is over the amount rather than coverage, the policy's appraisal clause or TWIA's statutory appraisal process may also be an option; see <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a>. Deadlines apply, so check your policy and use the <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a>; consult a Texas attorney about any deadline to file suit.</p>

<h2>How Our Service Process Works</h2>
<p>Every claim is different, but our approach is consistent:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Free Claim Review.</strong> We talk through the loss, the policies involved, and where the claim stands.</li>
<li><strong>Policy review.</strong> We read the declarations, forms, endorsements, and deductibles that apply.</li>
<li><strong>Inspection and documentation.</strong> We document the damage with photos, measurements, drone images, and Matterport 3D scans where useful.</li>
<li><strong>Estimate.</strong> We prepare a detailed line-item estimate in Xactimate.</li>
<li><strong>Presentation and negotiation.</strong> We submit the claim or supplement, attend reinspections, and handle communication with the insurer.</li>
<li><strong>Resolution.</strong> We stay involved through payment, recoverable depreciation, and any remaining disputed items.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When Professional Representation Makes Sense</h2>
<p>You do not need a public adjuster for every claim. Consider getting help if the claim is delayed or disputed, the settlement offer does not cover repairs, the insurer keeps asking for more information, more than one policy is involved, or the damage is extensive or hidden. A public adjuster in Corpus Christi can step in at nearly any stage, though earlier involvement often prevents missteps. We cannot promise a particular outcome, and public adjusters do not give legal advice. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
